Selected Idea
-------------

A generic "preserve editorial intent" instruction failed: prompt-level guardrails didn't prevent editorial drift
A small, generic addition to the Narrative user prompt that asked the agent to preserve Synthesis editorial hierarchy did not keep the intended company-level emphasis. Instead, candidate runs consistently demoted Nu�yon's company identity/audience and elevated orchestration into the central message, while also introducing unsupported audience and validation claims. The experiment concluded the candidate prompt must not be promoted and production was restored.

Research
--------

### Facts
1. Date: 2026-11-04.
2. Decision: Candidate not promoted; production Narrative user prompt restored byte-for-byte to baseline.
3. Objective: Test whether a reusable editorial-hierarchy rule in the Narrative Agent prompt keeps Synthesis's primary message, central subject, intended audience, and theme hierarchy intact.
4. The expected result was a company introduction centered on Nu�yon's AI-first ambition for other AI-first businesses, with Nu�yon.Compose as its first concrete product direction and orchestration as supporting explanation.
5. The frozen baseline Narrative system prompt and user prompt matched the production versions before the experiment; the baseline user prompt SHA-256 was 475A9DB1CA028EBC927C99ECDD3DAB061B0616ED06FB1278879992D2E8DD795A.
6. All 11 prompt files in the prior Experiment 004 baseline snapshot matched the current production prompt files by SHA-256.
7. No Research, Synthesis, Compose, Idea, configuration, or C# file was changed.
8. Baseline condition reused three completed baseline runs from experiment-004-opening-and-ending/baseline to preserve the prior baseline condition.
9. Candidate condition executed three new runs with only src/Nueyon.Compose.Application/Prompts/Narrative/Narrative.user.prompt.txt changed; all other prompt files and workflow inputs remained identical.
10. Provider/model: OpenAI / gpt-5-mini; .NET SDK 10.0.401; temperature and seed not explicitly configured; API key configured through user secrets and not recorded.
11. Compose format used: LinkedInPost.
12. The application reads Markdown files from data/input and has no separate runtime argument for original-instruction.txt.
13. Each run completed all workflow stages and exited 0; full console logs, extracted Narrative outputs, and final Compose outputs are saved under this experiment's baseline/outputs/ and candidate/outputs/.
14. The candidate prompt added one generic section titled "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis." 
15. That section tells Narrative to identify the primary message, supporting themes, and contextual details; preserve audience scope and relative emphasis; keep technical details subordinate when Synthesis does; and retain uncertainty when the source is ambiguous.
16. The added section contains no Nu�yon-specific wording or this story's details.
17. Narrative scores by condition: baseline mean 6.00/10 (range 5-7; sample SD 1.00); candidate mean 4.00/10 (range 4-4; sample SD 0.00).
18. Compose scores by condition: baseline mean 5.67/10 (range 4-7; sample SD 1.53); candidate mean 3.33/10 (range 3-4; sample SD 0.58).
19. Candidate Narrative outputs were readable and coherent but consistently subordinated company identity and audience scope while making orchestration the central positioning.
20. Candidate Narrative samples introduced unsupported validation or audience framing.
21. Candidate final Compose outputs remained readable but did not restore the intended editorial hierarchy.
22. Candidate Compose run 1 included editorial meta-text ("The post's purpose is simple") and a claim that the workflow would be validated before expansion.
23. Candidate Compose run 2 generalized about what useful AI requires and named founders/product leaders.
24. Candidate Compose run 3 opened by reframing content creation as an orchestration problem and expanded to architecture tradeoffs and validation focus.
25. All three candidate Narrative runs and all three candidate Compose runs failed the gate that orchestration remain a supporting explanation (criterion C).
26. No candidate run passed the factual/editorial restraint gate (criterion D).
27. The experiment report states: "Candidate was worse on mean score for both Narrative and Compose, failed the orchestration hierarchy and factual restraint gates in every run, and introduced regressions." 
28. Build and tests after rollback: dotnet build src/Nueyon.Compose.slnx - passed, 0 warnings, 0 errors; dotnet test src/Nueyon.Compose.slnx --no-build - 165 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.
29. Rollback verification: production prompt SHA-256 475A9DB1CA028EBC927C99ECDD3DAB061B0616ED06FB1278879992D2E8DD795A matches the frozen baseline exactly.
30. No commit or push was made.
31. Each condition had three runs.
32. Limitation: Baseline runs were produced on 2026-11-03; candidate runs were produced on 2026-11-04; the model provider may change the backend behind an alias.
33. Limitation: No seed or temperature override was available; three runs per condition show consistency in these samples, not statistical significance.
34. Limitation: Idea and Research outputs vary between runs; the console captures the selected first Idea, not every Idea candidate.
35. The baseline Synthesis pairs company identity with Compose in its top-level emphasis and also contains a later description calling orchestration "central to the product direction." 
36. The report states: "This internal tension may have reinforced the technical angle." 
37. The report states: "This does not change the controlled result: the candidate prompt was not sufficient to protect the supplied editorial intent, and this experiment did not change Synthesis because that was outside scope."

### Interpretations
1. [derived from Facts 17,18,25,26] The candidate prompt addition coincided with lower average scores for Narrative and Compose and with repeated failures of the orchestration-supporting-explanation gate and the factual/editorial-restraint gate.
2. [derived from Facts 27,29,30] The experiment's decision to not promote and to restore the production prompt was taken after observing worse mean scores, gate failures, and regressions, and rollback verification confirmed the baseline prompt was restored.

### Unknowns
1. The specific mechanism(s) by which the candidate prompt addition produced the observed editorial drift (e.g., why orchestration became central) is UNKNOWN.
2. Whether the baseline Synthesis's internal tension (Facts 35-36) caused, contributed to, or amplified the candidate outputs' technical emphasis is UNKNOWN.
3. Whether different wording, placement, or specificity of an editorial-intent guardrail would have prevented the observed drift is UNKNOWN.
4. Whether additional runs, temperature/seed control, or a different model backend would materially change the observed results is UNKNOWN.
5. Whether the candidate prompt change would produce similar failures for different source inputs, different audiences, or other editorial tasks is UNKNOWN.
6. Whether the candidate prompt introduced any unintended prompt-engineering errors (beyond content/clarity) that affected model behavior is UNKNOWN.
7. Whether the observed unsupported claims (validation/commitment language and named audience) reflect a reproducible bias in the model when presented with such a generic instruction is UNKNOWN.

### Development sequence
1. Initial plan/starting point: Objective defined to test whether a reusable editorial-hierarchy rule in the Narrative Agent prompt preserves Synthesis editorial hierarchy (Fact 3).
2. Baseline verification: Baseline prompt and repository state were verified; baseline prompts matched production by SHA-256 (Facts 5,6,7).
3. Baseline runs: Three baseline runs were reused from a prior experiment to preserve baseline condition (Facts 8,31).
4. Candidate change: The candidate Narrative user prompt was modified to add a generic section titled "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis" that instructs Narrative to preserve message, audience scope, emphasis, subordinate technical details, and uncertainty (Facts 14,15,16,9).
5. Candidate runs: Three candidate runs were executed with only that prompt change; each run completed the workflow (Facts 9,13,31).
6. Observed outputs and scoring: Candidate Narrative mean 4.00/10 vs baseline 6.00/10; candidate Compose mean 3.33/10 vs baseline 5.67/10; candidate outputs subordinated company identity and elevated orchestration; candidate runs introduced unsupported additions (Facts 17-21,23-26).
   Relationship between the prompt change (step 4) and the observed outputs/scores (step 6): UNKNOWN.
7. Decision and rollback: The report records the decision not to promote the candidate prompt, production prompt restored byte-for-byte, rollback SHA verified, and no commit/push made (Facts 2,27,29,30).
8. Limitations noted after the run: timing of baseline vs candidate runs, lack of seed/temperature control, small sample size, and possible model-backend variability (Facts 32-34).

